      "Who Is a Broker-Dealer",
      "The state Administrator also oversees broker-dealers and the people who work for them.\n\nUnder the USA, a broker-dealer (BD) is a business that effects securities transactions for its own account or for others. Like advisers, broker-dealers are usually companies, not individuals. They hire individuals to act as their agents.\n\nInvestment advisers are not broker-dealers. Advisers sell advice for a fee. They do not effect securities transactions.\n\nBroker-dealers do not have to register as investment advisers either. A broker-dealer may make recommendations, but it charges for the trade, not for the advice.\n\nThese are NOT broker-dealers under the USA:\n• Agents: individuals who effect trades for a broker-dealer. Agents may also be called registered representatives.\n• Issuers: corporations, cities, or governments that sell their own stocks or bonds in the primary market to raise money.\n• Banks, savings institutions, and trust companies: they are overseen by bank regulators instead of securities regulators.\n\nSubsidiaries of bank holding companies that trade securities with the public must register as broker-dealers. Commercial banks themselves are exempt.",
