      "s63-c2-bdstate",
      "When a Broker-Dealer Must Register in a State",
      "A broker-dealer with an office in a state, or doing business there, generally must register there. A broker-dealer with no office in a state does not have to register if it only deals with institutional clients. Institutional clients, also called institutional buyers, include:\n• Issuers\n• Other broker-dealers\n• Banks, savings institutions, and trust companies\n• Insurance companies\n• Investment companies, such as mutual funds, UITs, and closed-end funds\n• Pension or profit-sharing trusts\n• Anyone else the Administrator names\n\nThere is no de minimis exemption for broker-dealers. If a broker-dealer with no office in a state has even one retail client there, it must register there.\n\nExample: A broker-dealer has an office in State A, so it registers in State A. It has no office in State B but sells to retail clients there, so it registers in State B. It has no office in State C and only sells to institutions there, so it does not register in State C.\n\nA broker-dealer with no office in a state does not need to register there to serve a client who is only visiting. But if the client moves there for good and the firm wants to keep serving them, it must register. The same rule applies to agents.",
