      "s63-c2-bdfederal",
      "Registering With the SEC, FINRA, and SIPC",
      "Broker-dealers usually register with the SEC and also meet state rules. Two kinds can skip the SEC:\n• Intrastate broker-dealers, whose trades and clients are all in one state.\n• Broker-dealers that only trade certain exempt securities.\n\nBroker-dealers register with the SEC by filing Form BD through the Central Registration Depository (CRD). Form BD covers key people, contact facts, and any past violations. The SEC must approve it within 45 days or start steps to deny it.\n\nThen the broker-dealer registers with a self-regulatory organization (SRO), usually FINRA. It usually must also join the Securities Investor Protection Corporation (SIPC). SIPC protects up to $500,000 of customer assets if the firm goes bankrupt, with a $250,000 limit on cash. SIPC does not cover losses from investments going down.\n\nAn adviser registers with either the SEC or the states. A broker-dealer almost always registers with both the SEC and the states where it does business. A broker-dealer usually registers with FINRA too, but an investment adviser never does.",
